[ ] Media stack\: Usenet and torrents into a media dir, served by Jellyfin =media-stack $Darrion's vision, 2026-10-24: an alternative to the streaming services. Newshosting (the Usenet provider) plus nzbget fetch from Usenet, a torrent client adds the rest, both write into one media directory on this machine's storage, and Jellyfin serves it. nzbget runs on the desktop for now while Darrion learns it; the downloaders belong here, since the desktop sleeps. Notes for when it starts: the hardware may be an old ThinkStation instead of the N150 (Darrion, not decided); media is re-downloadable, so the RAID1 mirror is enough and it stays out of the B2 and Drive backups; the torrent client should only reach the network through a VPN, since a swarm sees its IP; nzbget's default web login must change. Start minimal (no Sonarr or Radarr until manual use shows the need). Ethics, open (2026-10-24): Darrion pays for media and wants higher-quality copies that are theirs to keep, and is unsure how to do that ethically, which is why this has never been top priority. Discussed, cleanest first: rip owned discs (UHD Blu-ray beats streams; MakeMKV), DRM-free purchases, copies of things already bought, then unpaid downloads (not wanted). Candidate rule, not adopted: only keep what I've paid for. Not a priority now.$ !4 ^2026-10-24T21:59 <services-up #01a16fed-079c-7470-bf80-5a5637773114
[ ] Bring the N150 up on personal-os with its carried services =services-up $The gate that work across projects waits on, until now only named in prose (found 2026-11-04 by the facilitator: about ten open actions in rwd, chezmoi, personal-os and clearhead-core say 'once personal-os is flashed' or 'blocked until the N150 runs...'). Done when the N150 boots the services image and runs the services the services charter carries from mini-server: SSH, Tailscale, Syncthing, Radicale, the OpenTelemetry Collector, vdirsyncer and restic, with the RAID1 storage at /srv/storage. +human: disk writes need Darrion's explicit approval, and flashing is physical. Dependents link to this action with waitsOn.$ !2 +human ^2026-11-04T07:38 #01a1a57e-d20e-72c1-92b2-599cce9797ea
[ ] Merge Syncthing conflict copies of plaintext files on the services machine =syncthing-merge $Darrion, 2026-11-04: Syncthing is ClearHead's sync, keeping sync an ops problem with files as the only truth (platform Decision 31's filesystem boundary). Syncthing never merges: the older of two concurrent edits becomes name.sync-conflict-<date>-<device>.ext. A watcher on the always-on N150 only (a second merger could race it) runs git merge-file over the current file, the conflict copy and a base the watcher keeps itself as the last version it saw (not .stversions, which skips local edits). On a clean merge it writes the result and deletes the copy; otherwise it leaves both. Never --union for .actions (it would revive archived lines). Starting point to read, not run blind: https://gist.github.com/Seas0/f4fcd181cc51ab1f1766d7d583bc0cb0 . Acceptance: a three-device test (phone, desktop, server) with offline edits to different actions merging cleanly, and the same action changed on both sides left as a conflict. No ClearHead code.  2026-11-04: once platform =semantic-merge exists, the watcher calls it for .actions files instead of git merge-file, which conflicts on neighbouring lines.$ !3 ^2026-11-04T07:38 <services-up #01a1a57e-d217-7c32-ab1b-e0676fc5dd6f
